+[F11] On smooth curves the covariant derivative is the pullback connection ([[def-covariant-derivative-along-a-curve]]). For $C^1$ fields $V$ along a $C^1$ curve, use $(D_sV)^a:=dV^a/ds+\Gamma^a{}_{bc}(x)(dx^b/ds)V^c$. This extends the smooth pullback definition: under a smooth frame change with matrix $C(s)$, $v=Cw$ and $B_{\rm new}=C^{-1}BC+C^{-1}C'$ give $v'+Bv=C(w'+B_{\rm new}w)$, so the formula is intrinsic and uses only first derivatives. The calculation in [[prop-local-frame-formula-for-covariant-differentiation-along-a-curve]] Proof 2.1 therefore applies at this regularity. The component metric-compatibility calculation of [[lem-gr-parallel-transport-and-geodesic-speed]] Proof 1.1–2.1 gives $d\,g(V,W)/ds=g(D_sV,W)+g(V,D_sW)$ for $C^1$ fields and curves, with one-sided derivatives at included endpoints. For a $C^2$ variation, these formulas in each parameter direction and commuting mixed partials define the derivatives used below.
 
